<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
SPDX-FileCopyrightText: © 2026 Jeffrey C. Ollie <jeff@ocjtech.us>
SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

Two things a Win32 program has to ask for and does not get by default.

Version 6 of the common controls is the first: without this dependency the
combo box, the up-down and the button are drawn in the Windows 95 style, and
`TTS_*` tooltips do not appear at all. Asking for it by assembly name is the
only way, as there is no API call that turns it on.

DPI awareness is the second. `true` is system awareness, which is what this
program implements: it reads `GetDpiForSystem` once and lays the controls out
in scaled units. Claiming `PerMonitorV2` would be a lie, since nothing here
handles `WM_DPICHANGED`, and a window dragged to a second monitor would then
be left at the wrong size rather than being bitmap-scaled to roughly the right
one.

Note the absence of the double hyphen this repository otherwise writes for an
em dash. XML forbids it inside a comment, and a manifest that is not
well-formed XML is not reported as a bad manifest: the program simply fails to
start, saying that its side-by-side configuration is incorrect.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="dev.jcollie.fluent.Greeting"
      version="1.0.0.0"
      processorArchitecture="*"/>
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
          type="win32"
          name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
          version="6.0.0.0"
          processorArchitecture="*"
          publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
          language="*"/>
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
